<h2>Two tracks, one child</h2>
<p>A POCSO complaint sets two parallel processes in motion. The criminal track runs from FIR through investigation to trial before the Special Court. The protection track runs before the Child Welfare Committee — a district-level quasi-judicial body under Section 27 of the JJ Act, 2015, with the powers of a bench of Magistrates for matters concerning children in need of care and protection. The tracks are connected but distinct: the Special Court punishes; the CWC protects.</p>

<h2>The CWC's entry point: the 24-hour report</h2>
<p>Under the POCSO Rules, 2020, where the police receive information about an offence and the child is without family support, or is in a shelter, or the accused is within the household, or the child otherwise appears to need care and protection, the matter must be placed before the CWC within twenty-four hours. The CWC then conducts an assessment — speaking to the child, the family and the support agencies — and decides the immediate question that the criminal court cannot: where does this child safely live while the case proceeds?</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Report to CWC</strong> — by police/SJPU within 24 hours in the situations the Rules specify.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Assessment</strong> — the child's safety, the household, the risk from the accused, schooling and health needs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interim placement</strong> — with the family where safe; otherwise with a fit relative, foster care or a children's home.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Ongoing supervision</strong> — periodic review, counselling linkage, coordination with the District Child Protection Unit.</div>
</div>

<h2>The support person: the system's connective tissue</h2>
<p>The POCSO Rules provide for a support person to be assigned to the child, ordinarily through the CWC, drawn from trained social workers or recognised organisations. The support person's role spans the life of the case: preparing the child for statements and testimony without influencing content, attending proceedings, tracking dates, coordinating medical care and counselling, and assisting with interim and final compensation applications. In Delhi, the institutional framework for support persons has grown steadily, and Special Courts expect to be informed whether one has been appointed. For defence counsel, the support person's presence is not an adversary — it is part of the statutory design ensuring the child's participation is voluntary and informed.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Families sometimes perceive the CWC's involvement as interference. In practice, engagement works better than resistance: attending assessments, proposing safe custodial arrangements within the family, and cooperating with counselling directions usually leads the CWC to the least intrusive order consistent with the child's safety.</p></div>

<h2>Where the CWC and the Special Court meet</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Function</th><th>CWC</th><th>Special Court</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Child's custody and placement during the case</td><td>Primary authority</td><td>May be informed; can flag concerns</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Support person appointment</td><td>Assigns and supervises</td><td>Monitors availability at trial</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Compensation</td><td>Facilitates applications and documents</td><td>Awards interim and final compensation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trial, bail, sentence</td><td>No role</td><td>Exclusive jurisdiction</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rehabilitation after the case</td><td>Continuing jurisdiction under the JJ Act</td><td>None</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Confirm early whether the case has been reported to the CWC and what orders exist — custody directions affect access to the child for every purpose.</p>
  <p>Ask whether a support person has been assigned; if not, the omission is worth raising.</p>
  <p>Route compensation groundwork — documents, expenses, schooling needs — through the support person and CWC record.</p>
  <p>Remember the CWC's orders are themselves amenable to challenge under the JJ Act hierarchy where they overreach.</p>
</div>
<p>The CWC is the part of the POCSO architecture that outlasts the verdict: whatever happens at trial, the child's care and rehabilitation remain its charge. Practitioners who engage with it early serve both the case and the child better. This article is general information and not legal advice in any individual matter.</p>
